<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    kuna issue testcase: a predicated ARM instruction's condition skip is not
    filed as a jump to the next instruction in the cross-reference index.

    Issue:      kuna-analysis-armcondskip - `kuna xrefs` from any
                `movlt`/`ldrne`/`moveq` of an A32 or Thumb-2 image answered a
                Jump to the next instruction, which no other tool files.
    Stage:      P1 code/data partition - the on-demand cross-reference query
                (`listing/xrefs.rs`).

    The Listing tier and the xref query are built only on the real-object
    bootstrap path, never on the console decompile path (docs/spec/
    01-program-prep.md section 1.6), and no console command prints an xref
    row, so a stage test cannot assert the row itself. This case pins that
    the rule is inert in the rendered C of `clamp`, whose `movlt`/`movge`
    pair is the instruction shape it concerns. The row-level proof lives in
    the cargo gate `decompiler/crates/kuna-console/tests/verify_xreffp.rs`,
    over the `xreffp_arm_le32` fixture this case also loads.

    The XML wrapper only supplies the harness architecture; the pass clears it
    and loads the real linked ARM fixture (built from
    decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/xreffp_arm_le32.c).
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>clear architecture</com>
  <com>load file decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/xreffp_arm_le32</com>
  <com>read symbols</com>
  <com>load function clamp</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="KUNA-ARMCONDSKIP #1: clamp's first predicated pair renders as a comparison" min="1" max="1">^  if \(a0 &lt; a1\)$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="KUNA-ARMCONDSKIP #2: clamp's second predicated pair renders as an assignment" min="1" max="1">^    a0 = a2;$</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
